            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="true" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <HouseholdOperatingConditions numberOfOccupants="5" coolingSeasonMonths="0" isClothesWasherEnergySaver="true" isLightingEnergySaver="true" areAppliancesEnergySaver="false">
                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="20" heatingNighttime="18.3" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="3" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="3" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, the air leakage from your home is not too bad, and a certain amount of natural or mechanical air exchange in a house is required to maintain healthy indoor air quality. Overall, I would like to see it about 25% tighter, however the design of older house sections, where additions are added many times make this difficult to achieve.



I do however see an opportunity to reduce air leakage in conjunction with some other upgrade opportunities. Specifically, you would eliminate both furnace chimneys if you upgrade to the 95% AFUE propane furnaces and the open combustion air supply pipe for the Olson furnace. This should reduce overall air leakage &amp; heat loss from your home by at least 10%.



Some other smaller air leakage in your house is from around &amp; through many of the electrical outlets &amp; wall switches located on exterior walls &amp; the 2nd floor bathroom window which did not seem to close tightly when we were there. For the electrical outlets, some of them had foam gaskets behind the cover plates and these should be located behind all of them. Newer electrical outlets are designed to prevent air from leaking through the outlet itself. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 306&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of the 1978 trailer section of your house are 2x3 walls with R8 batt insulation and there has been a previous renovation where an additional 2" (R8) of extruded polystyrene (EPS) rigid foam insulation has been added to the exterior - Total Nominal = R16. The 2003 addition has 2x6 walls with R20 batt insulation and an additional 1.5" (R7.5) of extruded polystyrene (XTPS) rigid foam insulation added to the exterior = Total Nominal = R27.5. If this house were being constructed today, the walls would have R30. For your interest, I have modeled the expected energy savings if you were to increase the wall insulation of the older 1978 trailer (including the wall that adjoins the cold porch) from R16 up to R30. Although a good energy upgrade to complete, you will see that upgrading the furnaces and hot water tanks have a much better payback.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls of the 1978 trailer up to a minimum R30 insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 243&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 645 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. The exterior walls of the 1978 trailer represent about 34.5% of the total exposed exterior walls surface area of the whole house.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has 2 x 182 litre electric hot water tanks (one serving each of sides 32A &amp; 32B). If you are considering a switch to propane, we talked about an opportunity for you to consider replacing both of these  electric tanks with a higher continuous flow capacity ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your 2 electric hot water tanks with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,052&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

32A has an Olson oil furnace that is rated at 82.1% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE). 32B has an older Nordyne concentric flue (direct vent) model that has is about 83% Steady State Efficiency (SSE). The Olson furnace in 32A has a 5" chimney and also has a 5" open combustion air pipe to supply combustion air to that furnace.



I have modeled an upgrade to 2 x ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE: one to replace each of the two oil furnaces.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current oil furnaces with 2 high-efficiency condensing propane furnaces with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,863&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;53.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ($750 for each furnace).



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



As discussed while I was at your house, the Arctic Energy Alliance always recommends homeowners complete all energy conservation (eg: LED Bulbs, timer switches, etc.) &amp; energy efficiency (eg: heating &amp; hot water systems) measures before investing in renewable energy generation systems.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the right Southwest facing roof of the upper floor of 32A, flush mounted on the roof, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save about $ 859&lt;/b&gt; per year in electricity costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year in a hydro community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Biomass Heating&lt;/b&gt;



32A currently has a Harmon P43 wood pellet stove on the main floor. Based on the current furnace in 32A, I modeled the net energy savings (cost of pellets used less the cost of oil saved) if you were to use this pellet stove to offset about 50% of the heating requirements of that main floor. Estimated annual savings are about $732. To expand these savings, you could use the furnace fan to distribute this wood pellet heat throughout both floors of the house.



For 32B, you may want to consider installing a wood pellet stove in the rear living room section &amp; using the furnace in that side to distribute the wood pellet heat throughout 32B. Using this pellet stove about 50% of the time would add another $633 of estimated annual savings.



&lt;b&gt;Rebates:&lt;/b&gt;



If you were to install an eligible wood pellet stove in 32B, you would qualify for &lt;b&gt;1/3 of the purchase cost up to $ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home has the following current ventilation:



1. Range Hood Fan in 32A that vents to the outside (Range Hood in 32B does not vent to the outside)

2. 32A has 2 bath fans vented to the outside, and 32B has 1 bath fan vented to the outside.

3. There is a clothes dryer in each of 32A &amp; 32B and they are both vented to the outside.



I recommend you consider upgrading all exhaust fans to ENERGY STAR rated units, and ones that have an ultra low "sone" (noise) rating of 1.0 or less. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood in 32A, all 3 bathroom fans, and both dryers, indicated a pressure drop of 3.5 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home from the 5" chimney of the Olsen furnace in 32A. The pellet stove in 32A has a dedicated and sealed outside combustion air supply and the furnace in 32B has a direct vent "concentric" chimney vent with a built in dedicated combustion air supply and are much less susceptible to backdrafting combustion gases into the house.



During my assessment of your home, there was a lack of functioning smoke and/or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in both 32A &amp; 32B Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following Nov. 1, 2017 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Ken Baigent

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance&lt;/b&gt;

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
